The '''An-Ming Understanding''' (Zongese: 安明諒解; also known as the '''Treaty of 1605''' or the '''Haihia Conventions''') was a set of three political conventions signed between 1605 and 1606 in the Zongese port city of [[Haihia]]. Negotiated primarily between the [[Kingdom of Zong]] and the [[Sultanate of Maros]], the Understanding established zones of control in the New World, dividing the continents of [[Lurandia]] and [[Abyala]] between the two powers. The conventions were named after the two principal Zongese negotiators: [[Lim Ming]], a Zongese career diplomat, and [[Chen An]], a Shansi-born envoy who had risen to prominence in Zongese service.

The Understanding was concluded as a coordinated response to the expansion of colonial activity in the New World by both [[Daras|the Chengzhi Dynasty of Daras]] and [[Illypnian]] powers, particularly the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] and the [[Kingdom of Burawa]]. The conventions marked the formal entry of eastern Fosian states into the competition for New World territory and established a framework for Fosian claims that would later conflict with the existing [[Abyala Charter]] between Burawa and Volisania.

== Background ==

{{main|Great Disappointment|Jade Sea War}}

The discovery of the New World by Fosian maritime powers occurred independently of Illypnian exploration. Beginning in the late 14th century, following the conclusion of the [[Jade Sea War]] (c. 1300–1390), Zongese and other eastern Fosian navigators expanded eastward across the [[Shendan Ocean]], establishing contact with the western coast of the New World. The [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564 — the encounter between Volisanian explorers and established Fosian settlements on the western coast of the New World — revealed to Illypnian powers that the eastern Fosian states had preceded them in the colonisation of the new continents.

By the early 17th century, the colonial landscape of the New World had become increasingly contested. Illypnian powers, particularly Volisania and Burawa, had established a duopoly over the eastern seaboard of Abyala through the [[Abyala Charter]] of 1537. Meanwhile, Daras, seeking to recover from the trade blockades imposed following the Jade Sea War, had begun constructing a blue-water navy and dispatching colonial expeditions to the New World. Darasian settlements were established in the southernmost regions of Lurandia, the southern continent of the New World.

At the same time, on 1577 [[Sultanate of Maros]] under [[Sultan Jalaludin II]] seeking for expanding its sphere of influence to the New World after successfully established the settlement on southeastern [[Almuria]]. Having several expeditions and exploration to crossed Shendan Ocean several times Jalaludin II sought to establish a new trade route to the land on the northeast Shendan Ocean and the possibility to monopolize it.

The Kingdom of Zong, the dominant maritime power of eastern Fosia, viewed both Darasian and Illypnian colonial expansion with concern. The personal union between Zong and [[Bingzhou]] provided strategic depth, and the financial support of the [[Jyunhap|Shansi Confederation]] enabled sustained diplomatic and military engagement. The Sultanate of Maros, an Islamic thalassocracy on the Balausian Peninsula, had historically served as the frontier where Darasian eastward expansion was halted. Both powers recognised the necessity of a coordinated response to preserve Fosian interests in the New World.

== Negotiators ==
===Zong side===
The Understanding was named for the two principal negotiators who conducted the diplomacy on behalf of Zong.

==== Lim Ming ====

{{main|Lim Ming}}

'''Lim Ming''' (Zongese: 林明; born c. 1570s) was a Zongese career diplomat of the royal court. Rising through the ranks of the Zongese foreign service, Lim Ming specialised in negotiations with western Fosian states, particularly those bordering the Jade Sea. His experience in Darasian frontier diplomacy equipped him for the delicate task of negotiating with Maros, a state that had itself emerged from sustained conflict with the Chengzhi Dynasty.

==== Chen An ====

{{main|Chen An}}

'''Chen An''' (Shansi Cantonese: 陳安; born c. 1570s) was a diplomat of Shansi origin who rose to prominence in Zongese service. Born in the [[Saikit|Kingdom of Saikit]], a large Shansi state within the Jyunhap federation, Chen An entered Zongese diplomatic service as a young man and distinguished himself through his understanding of both Zongese and Marosan interests. His Shansi background provided him with linguistic and cultural fluency that proved invaluable in bridging Zongese and Marosan perspectives during the negotiations.



===Marosan side===
Marosan side is represented by the Marosan diplomat [[Marowa Sangaji]] and military strategist [[Daeng Pattingalloang]].

== Conventions ==

The An-Ming Understanding comprised three separate conventions, signed over 1605 and 1606 in Haihia.

=== First Convention: Zones of Control (1605) ===

The '''First Convention of Haihia''', signed in 1605 between Zong and Maros, established the fundamental partition of the New World between the two powers. The convention designated the [[Liaojie]] — the narrow isthmus connecting the continents of Lurandia and Abyala — as the boundary between the Zongese and Marosan spheres.

Under the terms of the First Convention:
* [[Lurandia]], the southern continent where Fosian navigators had first arrived, was assigned to the Zongese sphere of influence, with Zong and its constituent partners (including Bingzhou) granted sole access to resources and territorial claims.
* [[Abyala]], the northern continent, was assigned to the Marosan sphere, with the Sultanate of Maros granted sole access to resources and territorial claims.
* The [[Liaojie]] isthmus was designated as a neutral zone, with neither power permitted to establish permanent military fortifications.

The convention explicitly addressed the presence of Darasian colonies in southern Lurandia. The signatories agreed that the territory claimed by Daras was included within the Zongese sphere, and that Zong would pursue the matter through diplomatic and, if necessary, military means. The convention thus established a claim in principle to the entirety of Lurandia, regardless of Darasian presence.

Notably, the convention did not recognise Illypnian claims to Abyala established by the Abyala Charter of 1537. The Zongese and Marosan perspective was that the Charter was an agreement between Illypnian powers that had no binding authority over Fosian states. The First Convention thus established a competing legal framework for the partition of the New World.

=== Second Convention: Trade and Tariffs (1605–1606) ===

The '''Second Convention of Haihia''', negotiated between 1605 and 1606, established a framework for commercial access between the zones of control. The convention was signed by Zong, Maros, Bingzhou, and the Kingdom of Saikit, representing the broader coalition of eastern Fosian states with interests in the New World trade.

Under the terms of the Second Convention:
* Merchant vessels from all signatory states were permitted to trade in both Lurandian and Abyalan ports, subject to tariffs established by the host power.
* Tariff rates were standardised at 10 per cent of cargo value for goods traded between signatory states, reducing the potential for commercial friction.
* A joint commission, comprising representatives from each signatory, was established to adjudicate disputes arising from commercial activity in the New World.
* The signatories agreed to coordinate anti-piracy operations along the western coast of the New World, establishing a framework for the protection of shipping.

The inclusion of Saikit and Bingzhou as parties to the Second Convention reflected the broader coalition that Zong had assembled in support of its New World ambitions. The financial resources of the Shansi states and the strategic depth provided by Bingzhou were essential to the Zongese colonial project.

=== Third Convention: Land Transfers (1606) ===

The '''Third Convention of Haihia''', signed in 1606 between Zong and Maros, established a protocol for the transfer of territorial claims between Fosian states. The convention recognised that the initial partition might require adjustment as colonial activity progressed, and provided a legal mechanism for such adjustments.

Under the terms of the Third Convention:
* Signatory states were permitted to sell, cede, or exchange territorial claims in the New World with other Fosian states, provided that all transfers were registered with a joint commission.
* Transfers of claims to states outside the convention framework (including Darasian and Illypnian powers) were prohibited, ensuring that Fosian territorial claims would not be alienated to external powers.
* A right of first refusal was established: if a signatory state wished to divest itself of a territorial claim, it was required to offer the claim to the other signatory states before pursuing any other disposition.

The Third Convention ensured that the partition established by the First Convention remained flexible enough to accommodate changing circumstances, while preventing the fragmentation of Fosian claims through sale or cession to Illypnian or Darasian interests.

