== Significance ==

=== Fosian colonial framework ===

The An-Ming Understanding established the foundational legal framework for Fosian claims in the New World. By partitioning the continents between Zong and Maros, the conventions provided a coherent alternative to the Illypnian-dominated Abyala Charter system. The Understanding represented the formal entry of eastern Fosian states into the competition for New World territory as organised, treaty-bound claimants rather than as disparate settlers.

=== Conflict with Illypnian claims ===

The conventions directly conflicted with the existing colonial order established by the Abyala Charter. The Charter, signed in 1537 between Burawa and Volisania, had partitioned Abyala between the two Illypnian powers. The First Convention of Haihia, by assigning Abyala to the Marosan sphere, effectively repudiated the Charter's legitimacy from a Fosian perspective.

This conflict of claims would later manifest in armed conflict. The [[War of the Isthmus]] and subsequent colonial wars arose in part from the incompatible territorial frameworks established by the Abyala Charter and the An-Ming Understanding. The Illypnian powers viewed the Fosian conventions as illegitimate encroachments on territory already assigned by treaty; the Fosian powers viewed the Abyala Charter as an agreement between third parties with no binding authority over their own claims.

=== Darasian tensions ===

The conventions' treatment of Darasian colonies in southern Lurandia established a precedent for confrontation. By claiming the entirety of Lurandia for the Zongese sphere, the First Convention implicitly committed Zong to the eventual removal of Darasian settlements. This commitment would contribute to a series of colonial conflicts between Zong and the Chengzhi Dynasty, as Daras resisted the assertion of Zongese sovereignty over territories it had already settled.

=== Diplomatic innovation ===

The An-Ming Understanding marked a significant diplomatic achievement for the Kingdom of Zong. The successful negotiation of a comprehensive colonial framework with Maros demonstrated Zong's capacity to marshal the resources of its coalition—including the financial support of the Shansi states and the strategic depth of Bingzhou—in pursuit of a coordinated foreign policy. The conventions established Zong as the pre-eminent Fosian power in the competition for New World territory.

The role of Chen An, the Shansi-born diplomat who served as a bridge between Zongese and Marosan interests, exemplified the integration of the eastern Fosian states under Zongese leadership. The personal union between Zong and Bingzhou, and the financial and diplomatic support of the Jyunhap, enabled the coalition that produced the Understanding.

== Legacy ==

The An-Ming Understanding remained the governing framework for Fosian claims in the New World throughout the 17th century. The conventions provided the legal basis for Zongese expansion in Lurandia and Marosan activity in Abyala, and the mechanisms established by the Second and Third Conventions facilitated the coordination of Fosian colonial policy.

The conflict between the An-Ming Understanding and the Abyala Charter contributed to a series of colonial wars that would reshape the New World in the 17th and 18th centuries. The [[War of the Isthmus]], the [[Aethiopean War]] of the 1660s, and subsequent conflicts reflected the competing territorial frameworks established by the two treaty systems.

In modern historiography, the An-Ming Understanding is regarded as a landmark in the history of Fosian colonialism. The conventions demonstrated that eastern Fosian states could compete with Illypnian powers on equal terms, establishing legal frameworks and diplomatic mechanisms that mirrored those of the Illypnian colonial system. The Understanding represented the maturation of Fosian colonial ambition from ad hoc settlement to coordinated, treaty-bound expansion.

